1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:04,079
Section 1 chapter 8.

2
00:00:04,079 --> 00:00:07,318
From somewhere at the bottom of a passage the smell of roasting coffee—

3
00:00:07,318 --> 00:00:12,180
—real coffee, not Victory Coffee— —came floating out into the street.

4
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:14,700
Winston paused involuntarily.

5
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:19,260
For perhaps two seconds he was back in the half-forgotten world of his childhood

6
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:22,978
than a door banged, seeming to cut off the smell as abruptly as though it had

7
00:00:22,978 --> 00:00:24,959
been a sound.

8
00:00:24,959 --> 00:00:30,719
He had walked several kilometers over pavements, and his varicose ulcer was throbbing.

9
00:00:30,719 --> 00:00:33,959
This was the second time in three weeks that he had missed an evening at the

10
00:00:33,959 --> 00:00:37,920
Community center a rash act, since you could be certain that the number of your

11
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:40,920
attendances at the center was carefully checked.

12
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:46,079
In principle a Party member had no spare time, and was never alone except in bed.

13
00:00:46,079 --> 00:00:50,520
It was assumed that when he was not working, eating or sleeping he would be

14
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:55,680
taking part in some kind of communal recreation: to do anything that suggested a taste

15
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:00,478
for solitude, even to go for a walk by yourself, was always slightly dangerous.

16
00:01:00,478 --> 00:01:05,398
There was a word for it in Newspeak own life it was called, meaning individualism

17
00:01:05,398 --> 00:01:07,739
and eccentricity.

18
00:01:07,739 --> 00:01:11,398
But this evening as he came out of the Ministry the bar minist of the Aprilaire

19
00:01:11,398 --> 00:01:12,659
had tempted him.

20
00:01:12,659 --> 00:01:16,680
The sky was a warmer blue than he had seen it that year, and suddenly the long,

21
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:21,780
noisy evening at the center the boring, exhausting games, the lectures, the

22
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:25,978
creeking camaraderie oiled by gin, had seemed intolerable.

23
00:01:25,978 --> 00:01:29,520
On impulse he had turned away from the bus stop and wandered off into the

24
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:34,739
labyrinth of London, first south, then east, then north again, losing himself

25
00:01:34,739 --> 00:01:39,478
among unknown streets and hardly bothering in which direction he was going.

26
00:01:39,478 --> 00:01:43,739
'If there is hope,' he had written in the diary, 'it lies in the proles.'

27
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:49,139
The words kept coming back to him, statement of the mystical truth and a palpable absurdity.

28
00:01:49,139 --> 00:01:52,559
He was somewhere in the vague brown-colored slums to the north and

29
00:01:52,559 --> 00:01:55,859
east of what had once been sent pancreas Station.

30
00:01:55,859 --> 00:01:59,939
He was walking up a cobbled street of little two-story houses with battered

31
00:01:59,939 --> 00:02:03,840
doorways which gave straight on the pavement and which was somehow curiously

32
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:09,600
suggestive of rat holes There were puddles of filthy water here and there among the cobbles.

33
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:13,680
In and out of the dark doorways, and down narrow alleyways that branched off on

34
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:18,420
either side, people swarmed in astonishing numbers— —girls in full bloom,

35
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:22,620
with crudely lipsticked mouths, and youths who've chased the girls, and

36
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:26,219
swollen waddling women who showed you what the girls would be like in 10 years'

37
00:02:26,219 --> 00:02:31,200
time, and old bent creatures shuffling along on splayed feet, and ragged

38
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,559
barefooted children who played in the puddles and then scattered at angry

39
00:02:34,559 --> 00:02:36,540
yells from their mothers.

40
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:40,559
Perhaps a quarter of the windows in the street were broken and boarded up.

41
00:02:40,559 --> 00:02:45,359
Most of the people paid no attention to Winston; a few eyed him with a sort of guarded

42
00:02:45,359 --> 00:02:46,559
curiosity.

43
00:02:46,559 --> 00:02:50,459
Two monstrous women with brick red forearms folded across their aprons were

44
00:02:50,459 --> 00:02:52,559
talking outside a doorway.

45
00:02:52,559 --> 00:02:56,760
Winston caught scraps of conversation as he approached.

46
00:02:56,760 --> 00:03:00,239
'"Yes," I says to her that's all very well that says.

47
00:03:00,239 --> 00:03:04,379
"But if you'd have been in my place you'd have done the same as what I'd done. It's

48
00:03:04,379 --> 00:03:09,299
easy to criticize I says, "but you ain't got the same problems as what I got

49
00:03:09,299 --> 00:03:14,219
I said the other, 'that's just it. That's just where it is.'

50
00:03:14,219 --> 00:03:17,040
The strident voices stopped abruptly.

51
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:21,000
The women studied him in hostile silence as he went past.

52
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,439
But it was not hostility, exactly; merely a kind of weariness a momentary

53
00:03:25,439 --> 00:03:29,579
stiffening, as at the passing of some unfamiliar animal.

54
00:03:29,579 --> 00:03:34,139
The blue overalls of the Party could not be a common sight in a street like this.

55
00:03:34,139 --> 00:03:39,180
Indeed, it was unwise to be seen in such places, unless you had definite business there.

56
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:42,600
The patrols might stop you if you happen to run into them.

57
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:47,100
'May I see your papers, comrade? What are you doing here? What time did you leave

58
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:51,719
work? Is this your usual way home?'— ?'—and so on and so forth.

59
00:03:51,719 --> 00:03:55,500
Not that there was any rule against walking home by an unusual route:

60
00:03:55,500 --> 00:04:01,020
but it was enough to draw attention to you if the thoughts Police heard about it.

61
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:04,620
Suddenly the whole street was in commotion. There were yields of warning

62
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:09,299
from all sides. People were shooting into the doorways like rabbits. A young woman

63
00:04:09,299 --> 00:04:13,139
leapt out of doorway a little ahead of Winston, grabbed up a tiny child playing

64
00:04:13,139 --> 00:04:17,939
in a puddle, whipped her apron around it and lived back again, all in one movement.

65
00:04:17,939 --> 00:04:21,959
At the same instant a man in the concertina-like black suit, who had

66
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:27,600
emerged from a side alley ran towards Winston, pointing excitedly to the sky.

67
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:33,000
'Steamer healed Look out Governor Bang over Ed Lay down quick!'

68
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,799
'Steamer' was a nickname which, for some reason, the pros applied to rocket bombs.

69
00:04:37,799 --> 00:04:40,680
Winston promptly flung himself on his face.

70
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:44,579
The pearls were nearly always right when they gave you a warning of this kind.

71
00:04:44,579 --> 00:04:48,239
They seemed to possess some kind of instinct which told them several seconds

72
00:04:48,239 --> 00:04:54,239
in advance when a rocket was coming, although the rockets supposedly traveled faster than sound.

73
00:04:54,239 --> 00:04:57,359
Winston clasped his forearms above his head.

74
00:04:57,359 --> 00:05:01,620
There was a roar which seemed to make the pavement heave; a shower of light

75
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:03,780
objects patted onto his back.

76
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:09,659
When he stood up he found that he was covered with fragments of glass from the nearest window.

77
00:05:09,659 --> 00:05:15,239
He walked on a bomb had demolished a group of houses 200 meters up the street.

78
00:05:15,239 --> 00:05:19,859
A black plume of smoke hung in the sky, and below it a cloud of plaster dust in

79
00:05:19,859 --> 00:05:22,920
which a crowd was already forming around the ruins.

80
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:27,478
There was a little pile of plaster lying on the pavement ahead of him, and in the

81
00:05:27,478 --> 00:05:30,420
middle of it he could see a bright red streak.

82
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:34,859
When he got up to it he saw that it was a human hand severed at the wrist.

83
00:05:34,859 --> 00:05:41,459
Apart from the bloody stump, the hand was so completely white and as to resemble a plaster cast.

84
00:05:41,459 --> 00:05:45,600
He kicks the thing into the gutter, and then, to avoid the crowd, turned down a

85
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:47,520
side street to the right.

86
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:51,120
Within three or four minutes he was out of the area which the bomb had affected,

87
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:56,280
and the sordid swarming life of the streets was going on as though nothing had happened.

88
00:05:56,280 --> 00:06:00,840
It was nearly 20 hours, and the drinking shop switched the pearls frequented ('pubs',

89
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:03,659
they called them) were choked with customers.

90
00:06:03,659 --> 00:06:07,859
From their grimy swing doors, endlessly opening and shutting they came forth a

91
00:06:07,859 --> 00:06:10,859
smell of urine, sawdust and sour beer.

92
00:06:10,859 --> 00:06:15,780
In an angle formed by a projecting house front three men were standing very close together,

93
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:19,200
the middle one of them holding a folded up newspaper which the other two were

94
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:23,520
studying over his shoulder Even before he was near enough to make

95
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:28,559
out the expression on their faces, Winston could see absorption in every line of their bodies.

96
00:06:28,559 --> 00:06:32,340
It was obviously some serious piece of news that they were reading.

97
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:36,359
He was a few paces away from them when suddenly the group broke up and two of

98
00:06:36,359 --> 00:06:38,639
the men were in violent altercation.

99
00:06:38,639 --> 00:06:42,299
For a moment they seemed almost on the point of blows.

100
00:06:42,299 --> 00:06:47,040
'Can't you bleeding well listen to what I say? I tell you no number ending in seven

101
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,620
eight one for over 14 months!'

102
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:51,180
'Yes it has then!'

103
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:55,739
'No, it has not! Back home I got the old lot of them for over two years wrote

104
00:06:55,739 --> 00:06:57,659
down on a piece of paper.

105
00:06:57,659 --> 00:07:02,879
I take some down regular as the clock and I tell you, no number ending in seven—

106
00:07:02,879 --> 00:07:08,000
'Yes seven has one I could pretty near tell you the bleeding number

107
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,139
407 had ended in. It were in February— —second week in February.'

108
00:07:13,139 --> 00:07:17,040
'February your grandmother! I got it all down in black and white and I tell you,

109
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:20,280
no number I'll pack it in!' said the third man.

110
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:21,739
They were talking about the Lottery.

111
00:07:21,739 --> 00:07:27,659
Winston looked back when he had gone 30 meters They were still arguing, with vivid,

112
00:07:27,659 --> 00:07:29,520
passionate faces.

113
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:33,898
The Lottery, with its weekly payout of enormous prizes, was the one public event

114
00:07:33,898 --> 00:07:37,799
to which the proles paid serious attention. It was probable that there

115
00:07:37,799 --> 00:07:41,219
were some millions of Pros for whom the Lottery was the principal if not the

116
00:07:41,219 --> 00:07:43,318
only reason for remaining alive.

117
00:07:43,318 --> 00:07:48,780
It was their delight, their folly, their anodyne, their intellectual stimulant.

118
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:52,260
Where the Lottery was concerned, even people who could barely read and write

119
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:56,760
seemed capable of intricate calculations and staggering feats of memory.

120
00:07:56,760 --> 00:08:00,420
There was a whole tribe of men who made a living simply by selling systems,

121
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:03,120
forecasts and lucky amulets.

122
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:06,299
Winston had nothing to do with the running of the Lottery, which was managed

123
00:08:06,299 --> 00:08:10,680
by the Ministry of Plenty, but he was aware (indeed everyone in the Party was

124
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:13,920
aware) that the prizes were largely imaginary.

125
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:17,700
Only small sums were actually paid out, the winners of the big prizes being

126
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:19,739
non-existent persons.

127
00:08:19,739 --> 00:08:22,978
In the absence of any real intercommunication between one part of

128
00:08:22,978 --> 00:08:27,359
Oceania and another, this was not difficult to arrange.

129
00:08:27,359 --> 00:08:32,280
But if there was hope, it lay in the pearls You had to cling on to that.

130
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:36,000
When you put it in words it sounded reasonable: it was when you looked at the

131
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,139
human beings passing you on the pavement that it became an act of faith.

132
00:08:40,139 --> 00:08:44,579
The street into which he had turned ran downhill. He had a feeling that he had

133
00:08:44,579 --> 00:08:49,439
been in this neighborhood before, and that there was a main thoroughfare not far away.

134
00:08:49,439 --> 00:08:52,680
From somewhere ahead there came a din of shouting voices.

135
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:56,520
The street took a sharp turn and then ended in a flight of steps which led

136
00:08:56,520 --> 00:09:01,799
down into a sunken alley where a few stall Keepers were selling tired looking vegetables.

137
00:09:01,799 --> 00:09:04,620
At this moment Winston remembered where he was.

138
00:09:04,620 --> 00:09:08,879
The alley led out into the main street, and down the next turning, not five

139
00:09:08,879 --> 00:09:13,978
minutes away, was the junk shop where he had bought the blank book which was now his diary.

140
00:09:13,978 --> 00:09:17,939
And in a small stationess shop not far away he had bought his pen holder and

141
00:09:17,939 --> 00:09:20,120
his bottle of ink.

142
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:24,959
He paused for a moment at the top of the steps. On the opposite side of the alley

143
00:09:24,959 --> 00:09:28,680
there was a dingy little pub whose windows appeared to be frosted over but

144
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:31,379
in reality they were merely coated with dust.

145
00:09:31,379 --> 00:09:35,818
A very old man, bent but active, with white mustaches that bristled forward

146
00:09:35,818 --> 00:09:40,200
like those of a prawn, pushed open the swing door and went in.

147
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:44,340
As Winston stood watching it occurred to him that the old man, who must be 80 at

148
00:09:44,340 --> 00:09:48,600
the least, had already been middle-aged when the Revolution happened.

149
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:52,139
He and a few others like him were the last links that now existed with the

150
00:09:52,139 --> 00:09:54,478
vanished world of capitalism.

151
00:09:54,478 --> 00:09:58,379
In the Party itself there were not many people left whose ideas had been formed

152
00:09:58,379 --> 00:10:00,120
before the Revolution.

153
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:04,379
The older generation had mostly been wiped out in the great purges of the 50s

154
00:10:04,379 --> 00:10:08,579
and 60s and the few who survived had long ago been terrified into complete

155
00:10:08,579 --> 00:10:10,379
intellectual surrender.

156
00:10:10,379 --> 00:10:13,978
If there was anyone still alive who could give you a truthful account of

157
00:10:13,978 --> 00:10:18,239
conditions in the early part of the century, it could only be a pro

158
00:10:18,239 --> 00:10:21,840
Suddenly the passage from the history book that he had copied into his diary

159
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:26,520
came back into Winston's mind, and a lunatic impulse took hold of him.

160
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:31,620
He would go into the pub, he would scrape acquaintance with that old man and question him.

161
00:10:31,620 --> 00:10:36,120
He would say to him: 'Tell me about your life when you were a boy. What was it

162
00:10:36,120 --> 00:10:41,280
like in those days? Were things better than they are now, or were they worse?'

163
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:45,299
Hurriedly, lest he should have time to become frightened, he descended the steps

164
00:10:45,299 --> 00:10:49,920
and crossed the narrow street. It was madness, of course. As usual, there was no

165
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:54,000
definite rule against talking to proles and frequenting their pubs, but it was

166
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,939
far too unusual an action to pass unnoticed.

167
00:10:56,939 --> 00:11:01,139
If the patrols appeared he might plead an attack of faintness, but it was not

168
00:11:01,139 --> 00:11:03,420
likely that they would believe him.

169
00:11:03,420 --> 00:11:08,579
He pushed open the door, and a hideous cheesy smell of sour beer hit him in the face.

170
00:11:08,579 --> 00:11:12,719
As he entered the dinner voices dropped to about half its volume.

171
00:11:12,719 --> 00:11:17,939
Behind his back he could feel everyone eyeing his blue overalls. A game of darts

172
00:11:17,939 --> 00:11:21,059
which was going on at the other end of the room interrupted itself for perhaps

173
00:11:21,059 --> 00:11:23,459
as much as 30 seconds.

174
00:11:23,459 --> 00:11:27,719
The old man whom he had followed was standing at the bar, having some kind of

175
00:11:27,719 --> 00:11:33,898
altercation with the barman, a large, stout, hook-nosed young man with enormous forearms.

176
00:11:33,898 --> 00:11:39,600
A lot of others, standing around with glasses in their hands, were watching the scene.

177
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:43,978
'I asked you civil enough, didn't I?' said the old man, straightening his shoulders

178
00:11:43,978 --> 00:11:49,680
pugnaciously. 'You telling me you ain't got a pint mug in the old bleeding boozer?'

179
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:54,959
'And what in hell's name is a pint?' said the barman, leaning forward with the tips

180
00:11:54,959 --> 00:11:59,818
of his fingers on the counter architim Calls himself a barman and

181
00:11:59,818 --> 00:12:04,079
don't know what a pint is or he passed the half of a court and there's four

182
00:12:04,079 --> 00:12:08,818
quarts to the gallon I have to teach you the ABC next.'

183
00:12:08,818 --> 00:12:12,959
'Never heard of him said the barman shortly liter and half liter that's all

184
00:12:12,959 --> 00:12:17,219
we serve. There's the glasses on the shelf in front of you.'

185
00:12:17,219 --> 00:12:19,680
'I like the pint,' persisted the old man.

186
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:22,559
'You could have drawed me off a pint easy enough.

187
00:12:22,559 --> 00:12:26,760
We didn't have these bleeding leaders when I was a young man.'

188
00:12:26,760 --> 00:12:30,420
'When you were a young man we were all living in the treetops in the barman,

189
00:12:30,420 --> 00:12:32,398
with a glance at the other customers.

190
00:12:32,398 --> 00:12:37,978
There was a shout of laughter, and the uneasiness caused by Winston's entry seemed to disappear.

191
00:12:37,978 --> 00:12:42,959
The old man's white stubbled face had flushed pink. He turned away, muttering to

192
00:12:42,959 --> 00:12:45,120
himself, and bumped into Winston.

193
00:12:45,120 --> 00:12:48,659
Winston caught him gently by the arm.

194
00:12:48,659 --> 00:12:51,299
'May I offer you a drink?' he said.

195
00:12:51,299 --> 00:12:55,318
'You're a gent,' said the other, straightening his shoulders again. He

196
00:12:55,318 --> 00:13:01,139
appeared not to have noticed Winston's blue overalls but he added aggressively to the barman

197
00:13:01,139 --> 00:13:03,420
part of wallop.'

198
00:13:03,420 --> 00:13:07,620
The barman switched two half liters of dark brown beer into thick glasses which

199
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:10,379
he had rinsed in a bucket under the counter.

200
00:13:10,379 --> 00:13:13,620
Beer was the only drink you could get in parole pubs.

201
00:13:13,620 --> 00:13:16,920
The pearls were supposed not to drink gin, though in practice they could get

202
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:18,959
hold of it easily enough.

203
00:13:18,959 --> 00:13:22,799
The game of darts was in full swing again, and the knot of men at the bar had

204
00:13:22,799 --> 00:13:25,260
begun talking about lottery tickets.

205
00:13:25,260 --> 00:13:28,200
Winston's presence was forgotten for a moment.

206
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:32,040
There was a deal table under the window where he and the old man could talk

207
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:34,200
without fear of being overheard.

208
00:13:34,200 --> 00:13:37,739
It was horribly dangerous, but at any rate there was no telescreen in the room,

209
00:13:37,739 --> 00:13:42,180
a point he had made sure of as soon as he came in

210
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:45,840
he could have drawed me off a pint crumbled the old man as he settled down

211
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:51,359
behind A glass half liter ain't enough don't satisfy her old lead us too much.

212
00:13:51,359 --> 00:13:53,760
It starts my bladder running.

213
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:56,520
Let alone the price.'

214
00:13:56,520 --> 00:14:01,920
'You must have seen great changes since you were a young man,' said Winston tentatively.

215
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:05,939
The old man's pale blue eyes moved from the darts board to the bar, and from the

216
00:14:05,939 --> 00:14:09,780
bar to the door of the Gents, as though it were in the bar room that he expected

217
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:12,540
the changes to have occurred.

218
00:14:12,540 --> 00:14:18,719
'The beer was better,' he said finally. 'And cheaper! When I was a young man, mild beer—

219
00:14:18,719 --> 00:14:24,719
—wallop, we used to call it— —was four principine That was before the war, of course.'

220
00:14:24,719 --> 00:14:27,780
'Which war was that?' said Winston.

221
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:30,359
'It's all wars,' said the old man vaguely.

222
00:14:30,359 --> 00:14:35,700
He took up his glass, and his shoulders straightened again he is wishing you the very best of

223
00:14:35,700 --> 00:14:40,739
health In his lean throat the sharp pointed Adam's apple made a surprisingly rapid

224
00:14:40,739 --> 00:14:44,159
up and down movement, and the beer vanished.

225
00:14:44,159 --> 00:14:48,719
Winston went to the bar and came back with two more half liters The old man

226
00:14:48,719 --> 00:14:53,719
appeared to have forgotten his prejudice against drinking a full liter

227
00:14:53,818 --> 00:14:57,600
You are very much older than I am,' said Winston. 'You must have been a grown man

228
00:14:57,600 --> 00:15:03,000
before I was born. You can remember what it was like in the old days, before the Revolution.

229
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,420
People of my age don't really know anything about those times.

230
00:15:06,420 --> 00:15:10,079
We can only read about them in books, and what it says in the books may not be

231
00:15:10,079 --> 00:15:10,920
true.

232
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:15,120
I should like your opinion on that. The history books say that life before the

233
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:18,059
Revolution was completely different from what it is now.

234
00:15:18,059 --> 00:15:23,398
There was the most terrible oppression in Justice poverty— —worse than anything we can imagine.

235
00:15:23,398 --> 00:15:27,898
Here in London, the great mass of people never had enough to eat from birth to death.

236
00:15:27,898 --> 00:15:32,398
Half of them hadn't even boots on their feet. They worked 12 hours a day, they

237
00:15:32,398 --> 00:15:35,760
left school at nine, they slept 10 in a room.

238
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:39,600
And at the same time there were very few people, only a few thousands— —the

239
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:42,840
capitalists, they were called— —who were rich and powerful.

240
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:45,420
They owned everything that there was to own.

241
00:15:45,420 --> 00:15:49,680
They lived in great gorgeous houses with 30 servants, they wrote about in motor

242
00:15:49,680 --> 00:15:54,239
cars and four horse carriages, they drank champagne, they wore top hats———'

243
00:15:54,239 --> 00:15:56,939
The old man brightened suddenly.

244
00:15:56,939 --> 00:16:01,500
'Top hats he said. 'Funny you should mention him The same thing coming to my

245
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:03,600
head only yesterday, I don't know why.

246
00:16:03,600 --> 00:16:08,398
I was just thinking, I ain't seen the top hat in years gone right out, they have

247
00:16:08,398 --> 00:16:12,059
The last time I wore one was at my sister-in-law's funeral.

248
00:16:12,059 --> 00:16:17,280
And that was— —well, I couldn't give you the date, but it must have been 50 years ago.

249
00:16:17,280 --> 00:16:21,000
Of course it was only odd for the occasion, you understand.'

250
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:25,260
'It isn't very important about the top hats at Winston patiently.

251
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:29,219
'The point is, these capitalists— —they and a few lawyers and priests and so forth

252
00:16:29,219 --> 00:16:33,420
who lived on them— —were the lords of the earth. Everything existed for their

253
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:38,340
benefit. You— —the ordinary people, the workers— —were their slaves. They could do

254
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:39,719
what they liked with you.

255
00:16:39,719 --> 00:16:43,079
They could ship you off to Canada like cattle. They could sleep with your

256
00:16:43,079 --> 00:16:46,500
daughters if they chose. They could order you to be flogged with something called

257
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:48,540
a canine tails.

258
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:52,260
You had to take a cap off when you passed them. Every capitalist went about

259
00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:56,359
with a gang of lackeys who———' The old man brightened again.

260
00:16:56,359 --> 00:17:00,559
'Lackeys!' he said. 'Now there's a word I ain't heard since ever so long.

261
00:17:00,559 --> 00:17:07,139
Lackeys! That regular takes me back, that does. I recollect— —oh donkeys years ago—

262
00:17:07,139 --> 00:17:12,600
—I used to go sometimes toward Park of a Sunday afternoon to hear the blokes making speeches.

263
00:17:12,600 --> 00:17:18,420
Salvation Army, Roman Catholics, Jews, Indians— —all sorts, there was. And there

264
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:23,100
was one bloke— —well, I couldn't give you his name, but a real powerful speaker he was

265
00:17:23,100 --> 00:17:29,579
you didn't off give it him Lackeys he says, "Lackeys of the bourgeoisie! Flunkies

266
00:17:29,579 --> 00:17:31,260
of the ruling class!"

267
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:36,659
Parasites— —that was another of them. And hyenas he definitely called him hyenas

268
00:17:36,659 --> 00:17:40,200
Of course he was referring to the labor Party, you understand.'

269
00:17:40,200 --> 00:17:44,340
Winston had the feeling that they were talking at Cross purposes

270
00:17:44,340 --> 00:17:48,299
What I really wanted to know was this,' he said. 'Do you feel that you have more

271
00:17:48,299 --> 00:17:50,100
freedom now than you had in those days?

272
00:17:50,100 --> 00:17:52,280
Are you treated more like a human being?

273
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:57,719
In the old days, the rich people, the people at the top———' 'The House of Lords,' put

274
00:17:57,719 --> 00:18:00,059
in the old man reminiscently.

275
00:18:00,059 --> 00:18:04,799
'The House of Lords, if you like. What I am asking is, were these people able to

276
00:18:04,799 --> 00:18:09,120
treat you as an inferior, simply because they were rich and you were poor? Is it a

277
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:12,780
fact, for instance, that you had to call them "Sir" and take off your cap when you

278
00:18:12,780 --> 00:18:15,059
passed them?'

279
00:18:15,059 --> 00:18:17,579
The old man appeared to think deeply.

280
00:18:17,579 --> 00:18:21,959
He drank off about a quarter of his beer before answering.

281
00:18:21,959 --> 00:18:28,020
'Yes,' he said. 'They like you to touch your cap to him It showed respect, like. I

282
00:18:28,020 --> 00:18:30,659
didn't agree with it, myself, but I'd done it often enough.

283
00:18:30,659 --> 00:18:33,780
Had to, as you might say.'

284
00:18:33,780 --> 00:18:37,920
'And was it usual— —I'm only quoting what I've read in history books— —was it usual

285
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:41,939
for these people and their servants to push you off the pavement into the gutter?'

286
00:18:41,939 --> 00:18:44,939
'One of them pushed me once,' said the old man.

287
00:18:44,939 --> 00:18:47,639
'I recollected as if it was yesterday.

288
00:18:47,639 --> 00:18:51,359
It was the Boat Race night terribly rowdy they used to get on Boat Race

289
00:18:51,359 --> 00:18:55,439
night— —and I bumped into a young bloke on Shaftsbury Avenue.

290
00:18:55,439 --> 00:19:01,680
Quite a gent he was— —dress shirt, top hat black overcoat he was kind of zigzagging across the

291
00:19:01,680 --> 00:19:06,478
pavement, and I bumps into him accidentally like he says, "Why can't you look where you're

292
00:19:06,478 --> 00:19:12,478
going he says. I say do you think you've bought the bleeding pavement he says,

293
00:19:12,478 --> 00:19:17,340
"I'll twist your bloody head off if you get fresh with me." I says, "You're drunk.

294
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:22,859
I'll give you in charge in half a minute," I says and if you'll believe me he puts

295
00:19:22,859 --> 00:19:26,040
his hand on my chest and gives me a shove it's pretty near sent me under the

296
00:19:26,040 --> 00:19:29,879
wheels of a bus I was young in them days, and I was going

297
00:19:29,879 --> 00:19:33,719
to have fetched him one, only———' A sense of helplessness took hold of

298
00:19:33,719 --> 00:19:37,978
Winston. The old man's memory was nothing but a rubbish heap of details.

299
00:19:37,978 --> 00:19:41,579
One could question him all day without getting any real information.

300
00:19:41,579 --> 00:19:46,859
The Party histories might still be true, after a fashion: they might even be completely true.

301
00:19:46,859 --> 00:19:49,739
He made a last attempt.

302
00:19:49,739 --> 00:19:54,239
'Perhaps I've not made myself clear,' he said. 'What I'm trying to say is this. You

303
00:19:54,239 --> 00:19:58,920
have been alive a very long time; you lived half your life before the Revolution.

304
00:19:58,920 --> 00:20:02,340
In 1925, for instance, you were already grown up.

305
00:20:02,340 --> 00:20:07,079
Would you say, from what you can remember, that life in 1925 was better than it is

306
00:20:07,079 --> 00:20:13,020
now, or worse? If you could choose, would you prefer to live then or now?'

307
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:17,398
The old man looked meditatively at the dart sport He finished up his beer, more

308
00:20:17,398 --> 00:20:19,079
slowly than before.

309
00:20:19,079 --> 00:20:25,139
When he spoke it was with a tolerant philosophical Heir as though the beer had mellowed him.

310
00:20:25,139 --> 00:20:27,420
'I know what you expect me to say,' he said.

311
00:20:27,420 --> 00:20:30,659
'You expect me to say I'd sooner be young again.

312
00:20:30,659 --> 00:20:33,959
Most people would say they'd sooner be young, if you asked him

313
00:20:33,959 --> 00:20:37,920
You got your health and strength when you're young. When you get to my time of

314
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:39,719
life you ain't never well.

315
00:20:39,719 --> 00:20:44,159
I suffered something wicked from my feet, and my blood is just terrible.

316
00:20:44,159 --> 00:20:47,340
Six and seven times a night it has me out of bed.

317
00:20:47,340 --> 00:20:52,379
On the other hand there's great advantages in being an old man. You ain't got the same worries.

318
00:20:52,379 --> 00:20:56,700
No truck with women, and that's a great thing. I ain't had a woman for nearly on

319
00:20:56,700 --> 00:21:01,620
30 years if you'd credit it. Nor want to do what's more.'

320
00:21:01,620 --> 00:21:06,359
Winston sat back against the windowsill It was no use going on. He was about to

321
00:21:06,359 --> 00:21:09,540
buy some more beer when the old man suddenly got up and shuffled rapidly

322
00:21:09,540 --> 00:21:12,719
into the stinking urinal at the side of the room.

323
00:21:12,719 --> 00:21:16,020
The extra half liter was already working on him.

324
00:21:16,020 --> 00:21:20,279
Winston sat for a minute or two gazing at his empty glass, and hardly noticed

325
00:21:20,279 --> 00:21:23,398
when his feet carried him out into the street again.

326
00:21:23,398 --> 00:21:28,500
Within 20 years at the most, he reflected, the huge and simple question, 'Was life

327
00:21:28,500 --> 00:21:32,398
better before the Revolution than it is now?' would have ceased once and for all

328
00:21:32,398 --> 00:21:33,779
to be answerable.

329
00:21:33,779 --> 00:21:37,978
But in effect it was unanswerable even now, since the few scattered survivors

330
00:21:37,978 --> 00:21:42,180
from the ancient world were incapable of comparing one age with another.

331
00:21:42,180 --> 00:21:46,260
They remembered a million useless things, a quarrel with a workmate a hunt for a

332
00:21:46,260 --> 00:21:50,760
lost bicycle pump, the expression on a long dead sister's face, the swirls of

333
00:21:50,760 --> 00:21:55,260
dust on a windy morning 70 years ago: but all the relevant facts were outside

334
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:57,059
the range of their vision.

335
00:21:57,059 --> 00:22:01,920
They were like the ant, which can see small objects but not large ones.

336
00:22:01,920 --> 00:22:07,020
And when memory failed and written records were falsified— —when that happened, the claim of the

337
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:10,318
Party to have improved the conditions of human life had got to be accepted,

338
00:22:10,318 --> 00:22:14,579
because they did not exist, and never again could exist in his standard

339
00:22:14,579 --> 00:22:17,579
against which it could be tested.

340
00:22:17,579 --> 00:22:21,659
At this moment his train of thought stopped abruptly. He halted and looked up.

341
00:22:21,659 --> 00:22:27,180
He was in a narrow street, with a few dark little shops interspersed among dwelling houses

342
00:22:27,180 --> 00:22:30,719
Immediately above his head they hung three discolored metal balls which

343
00:22:30,719 --> 00:22:32,879
looked as if they had once been gilded.

344
00:22:32,879 --> 00:22:37,318
He seems to know the place. Of course! He was standing outside the junk shop where

345
00:22:37,318 --> 00:22:39,779
he had bought the diary.

346
00:22:39,779 --> 00:22:43,978
A twinge of fear went through him. It had been a sufficiently rash act to buy the

347
00:22:43,978 --> 00:22:47,760
book in the beginning, and he had sworn never to come near the place again.

348
00:22:47,760 --> 00:22:51,420
And yet the instant that he allowed his thoughts to wander, his feet had brought

349
00:22:51,420 --> 00:22:53,579
him back here of their own accord.

350
00:22:53,579 --> 00:22:57,420
It was precisely against suicidal impulses of this kind that he had hoped

351
00:22:57,420 --> 00:22:59,939
to guard himself by opening the diary.

352
00:22:59,939 --> 00:23:04,978
At the same time he noticed that although it was nearly 21 hours the shop was still open.

353
00:23:04,978 --> 00:23:08,520
With the feeling that he would be less conspicuous inside than hanging about on

354
00:23:08,520 --> 00:23:11,700
the pavement, he stepped through the doorway.

355
00:23:11,700 --> 00:23:15,898
If questioned, he could plausibly say that he was trying to buy razor blades.

356
00:23:15,898 --> 00:23:20,879
The proprietor had just lighted a hanging oil lamp which gave off an unclean but friendly smell.

357
00:23:20,879 --> 00:23:26,159
He was a man of perhaps 60 frail and Abode with a long, benevolent nose, and

358
00:23:26,159 --> 00:23:29,100
mild eyes distorted by thick spectacles.

359
00:23:29,100 --> 00:23:33,299
His hair was almost white, but his eyebrows were bushy and still black.

360
00:23:33,299 --> 00:23:37,020
His spectacles, his gentle, fussy movements and the fact that he was

361
00:23:37,020 --> 00:23:41,579
wearing an aged jacket of black velvet, gave him a vague air of intellectuality,

362
00:23:41,579 --> 00:23:46,318
as though he had been some kind of literary man, or perhaps a musician.

363
00:23:46,318 --> 00:23:50,340
His voice was soft, as though faded, and his accentless debased than that of the

364
00:23:50,340 --> 00:23:55,500
majority of prose I recognized you on the pavement,' he said

365
00:23:55,500 --> 00:24:00,059
immediately. 'You're the gentleman that bought the young lady's keepsake album.

366
00:24:00,059 --> 00:24:04,620
That was a beautiful bit of paper, that was. Cream-laid, it used to be called.

367
00:24:04,620 --> 00:24:10,559
There's been no paper like that made for— —oh, I dare say 50 years.'

368
00:24:10,559 --> 00:24:14,219
He appeared at Winston over the top of his spectacles.

369
00:24:14,219 --> 00:24:19,680
'Is there anything special I can do for you? Or did you just want to look around

370
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:25,439
I was passing,' said Winston vaguely. 'I just looked in. I don't want anything in particular.'

371
00:24:25,439 --> 00:24:30,299
'It's just as well,' said the other, 'because I don't suppose I could have satisfied you.'

372
00:24:30,299 --> 00:24:34,139
He made an apologetic gesture with his soft palm hand.

373
00:24:34,139 --> 00:24:39,420
'You see how it is; an empty shop, you might say. Between you and me, the antique

374
00:24:39,420 --> 00:24:41,459
trades just about finished.

375
00:24:41,459 --> 00:24:44,459
No demand any longer, and no stock either.

376
00:24:44,459 --> 00:24:49,260
Furniture, china, glass— —it's all been broken up by degrees.

377
00:24:49,260 --> 00:24:54,000
And of course the metal stuff's mostly been melted down haven't seen a brass candlestick in

378
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,180
years.'

379
00:24:56,279 --> 00:25:00,659
The tiny interior of the shop was in fact uncomfortably full, but there was

380
00:25:00,659 --> 00:25:03,299
almost nothing in it of the slightest value.

381
00:25:03,299 --> 00:25:07,439
The floor- space was very restricted, because all around the walls were

382
00:25:07,439 --> 00:25:12,180
stacked innumerable dusty picture frames In the window there were trays of nuts

383
00:25:12,180 --> 00:25:16,859
and bolts worn out chisels Pen Knives with broken blades, tarnished watches

384
00:25:16,859 --> 00:25:21,719
that did not even pretend to be in going order, and other miscellaneous rubbish.

385
00:25:21,719 --> 00:25:25,760
Only on a small table in the corner was there a litter of odds and ends—

386
00:25:25,760 --> 00:25:30,120
—lacquered snuff boxes agate broaches and the lag which looked as though they

387
00:25:30,120 --> 00:25:32,398
might include something interesting.

388
00:25:32,398 --> 00:25:36,299
As Winston wandered towards the table his eye was caught by a round, smooth

389
00:25:36,299 --> 00:25:41,040
thing that gleamed softly in the lamplight, and he picked it up.

390
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:45,420
It was a heavy lump of glass, curved on one side, flat on the other, making almost

391
00:25:45,420 --> 00:25:46,978
a hemisphere.

392
00:25:46,978 --> 00:25:50,939
There was a peculiar softness, as of rain water in both the color and the texture

393
00:25:50,939 --> 00:25:52,379
of the glass.

394
00:25:52,379 --> 00:25:56,520
At the heart of it, magnified by the curved surface, there was a strange, pink,

395
00:25:56,520 --> 00:26:01,739
convoluted object that recalled a rose or a sea anemone.

396
00:26:01,739 --> 00:26:04,739
'What is it Sid Winston, fascinated.

397
00:26:04,739 --> 00:26:07,559
'That's coral, that is,' said the old man.

398
00:26:07,559 --> 00:26:09,180
'It must have come from the Indian Ocean.

399
00:26:09,180 --> 00:26:12,540
They used to kind of embed it in the glass.

400
00:26:12,540 --> 00:26:15,299
That wasn't made less than a hundred years ago.

401
00:26:15,299 --> 00:26:17,939
More, by the look of it.'

402
00:26:17,939 --> 00:26:20,579
'It's a beautiful thing,' said Winston.

403
00:26:20,579 --> 00:26:24,299
'It is a beautiful thing,' said the other appreciatively. 'But there's not many

404
00:26:24,299 --> 00:26:26,760
that'd say so nowadays.'

405
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:28,500
He coughed.

406
00:26:28,500 --> 00:26:33,120
'Now, if it so happened that you wanted to buy it that it cost you four dollars.

407
00:26:33,120 --> 00:26:37,379
I can remember when a thing like that would have fetched eight pounds, and eight pounds was—

408
00:26:37,379 --> 00:26:40,439
—well, I can't work it out, but it was a lot of money.

409
00:26:40,439 --> 00:26:45,719
But who cares about genuine antiques nowadays— —even the few that's left?'

410
00:26:45,719 --> 00:26:50,818
Winston immediately paid over the four dollars and slid the coveted thing into his pocket.

411
00:26:50,818 --> 00:26:54,659
What appealed to him about it was not so much its beauty as the air it seemed to

412
00:26:54,659 --> 00:26:58,379
possess of belonging to an age quite different from the present one.

413
00:26:58,379 --> 00:27:03,540
The soft rain watery glass was not like any glass that he had ever seen.

414
00:27:03,540 --> 00:27:07,379
The thing was doubly attractive because of its apparent uselessness, though he

415
00:27:07,379 --> 00:27:11,040
could guess that it must once have been intended as a paperweight.

416
00:27:11,040 --> 00:27:15,600
It was very heavy in his pocket, but fortunately it did not make much of a bulge.

417
00:27:15,600 --> 00:27:21,180
It was a queer thing, even a compromising thing, for a Party member to have in his possession.

418
00:27:21,180 --> 00:27:26,159
Anything old, and for that matter anything beautiful, was always vaguely suspect.

419
00:27:26,159 --> 00:27:30,659
The old man had grown noticeably more cheerful after receiving the four dollars.

420
00:27:30,659 --> 00:27:35,600
Winston realized that he would have accepted three or even two.

421
00:27:35,818 --> 00:27:38,760
'There's another room upstairs that you might care to take a look at,' he said.

422
00:27:38,760 --> 00:27:45,059
'There's not much in it. Just a few pieces will do with the light if we're going upstairs.'

423
00:27:45,059 --> 00:27:49,559
He lit another lamp, and, with bowed back, led the way slowly up the steep and worn

424
00:27:49,559 --> 00:27:53,340
stairs and along a tiny passage, into a room which did not give on the street

425
00:27:53,340 --> 00:27:57,239
but looked out on a cobbled yard and a forest of chimney pots.

426
00:27:57,239 --> 00:28:00,779
Winston noticed that the furniture was still arranged as though the room were

427
00:28:00,779 --> 00:28:02,340
meant to be lived in.

428
00:28:02,340 --> 00:28:04,079
There was a strip of carpet on the floor,

429
00:28:04,079 --> 00:28:10,079
a picture or two on the walls, and a deep slatenly armed chair drawn up to the fireplace.

430
00:28:10,079 --> 00:28:15,299
An old-fashioned glass clock with a 12-hour face was ticking away on the mantelpiece.

431
00:28:15,299 --> 00:28:19,379
Under the window, and occupying nearly a quarter of the room, was an enormous bed

432
00:28:19,379 --> 00:28:22,379
with the mattress still on it.

433
00:28:22,379 --> 00:28:26,639
'We lived here till my wife died,' said the old man half apologetically.

434
00:28:26,639 --> 00:28:29,879
'I'm selling the furniture off by little and little.

435
00:28:29,879 --> 00:28:33,719
Now that's a beautiful mahogany bed, or at least it would be if you could get

436
00:28:33,719 --> 00:28:39,000
the bugs out of it. But I dare say you'd find it a little bit cumbersome.'

437
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:43,318
He was holding the lamp high up, so as to illuminate the whole room, and in the

438
00:28:43,318 --> 00:28:46,799
warm dim lights the place looked curiously inviting.

439
00:28:46,799 --> 00:28:49,978
The thought flitted through Winston's mind that it would probably be quite

440
00:28:49,978 --> 00:28:54,659
easy to rent the room for a few dollars a week, if he dared to take the risk.

441
00:28:54,659 --> 00:28:59,159
It was a wild, impossible notion, to be abandoned as soon as thought of; but the

442
00:28:59,159 --> 00:29:03,540
room had awakened in him a sort of nostalgia, a sort of ancestral memory.

443
00:29:03,540 --> 00:29:07,139
It seems to him that he knew exactly what it felt like to sit in a room like

444
00:29:07,139 --> 00:29:11,279
this, in an armchair beside an open fire with your feet in the fender and a

445
00:29:11,279 --> 00:29:15,359
kettle on the hob: utterly alone, utterly secure, with nobody

446
00:29:15,359 --> 00:29:19,500
watching you, no voice pursuing you, no sound except the singing of the kettle

447
00:29:19,500 --> 00:29:22,559
and the friendly ticking of the clock.

448
00:29:22,559 --> 00:29:25,799
'There's no telescreen!' he could not help murmuring.

449
00:29:25,799 --> 00:29:31,260
'Ah,' said the old man, 'I never had one of those things. Too expensive. And I never

450
00:29:31,260 --> 00:29:33,600
seemed to feel the need of it, somehow.

451
00:29:33,600 --> 00:29:37,379
Now that's a nice gate leg table in the corner there. Though of course you'd have

452
00:29:37,379 --> 00:29:40,799
to put new hinges on it if you wanted to use the flaps.'

453
00:29:40,799 --> 00:29:45,659
There was a small bookcase in the other corner, and Winston had already gravitated towards it.

454
00:29:45,659 --> 00:29:47,939
It contained nothing but rubbish.

455
00:29:47,939 --> 00:29:50,879
The handing down and destruction of books had been done with the same

456
00:29:50,879 --> 00:29:53,760
thoroughness in the parole quarters as everywhere else.

457
00:29:53,760 --> 00:29:57,540
It was very unlikely that there existed anywhere in Oceania a copy of a book

458
00:29:57,540 --> 00:30:00,059
printed earlier than 1960..

459
00:30:00,059 --> 00:30:04,079
The old man, still carrying the lamp, was standing in front of the pictures in a

460
00:30:04,079 --> 00:30:09,420
rosewood frame which hung on the other side of the fireplace, opposite the bed.

461
00:30:09,420 --> 00:30:14,520
'Now, if you happen to be interested in old prints at all———' he began delicately.

462
00:30:14,520 --> 00:30:18,719
Winston came across to examine the picture. It was a steel engraving of an

463
00:30:18,719 --> 00:30:22,859
oval building with rectangular windows, and a small tower in front.

464
00:30:22,859 --> 00:30:26,520
There was a railing running around the building, and at the rear end there was

465
00:30:26,520 --> 00:30:28,620
what appeared to be a statue.

466
00:30:28,620 --> 00:30:31,139
Winston gazed at it for some moments.

467
00:30:31,139 --> 00:30:35,879
It seemed vaguely familiar, though he did not remember the statue.

468
00:30:35,879 --> 00:30:39,779
'The frames fixed to the wall,' said the old man, 'but I could unscrew it for you, I

469
00:30:39,779 --> 00:30:41,760
dare say.'

470
00:30:41,760 --> 00:30:46,020
'I know that building,' said Winston finally. 'It's a ruin now. It's in the

471
00:30:46,020 --> 00:30:49,020
middle of the street outside the Palace of Justice.'

472
00:30:49,020 --> 00:30:55,318
'That's right. Outside the Law Courts. It was bombed in— —oh, many years ago. It was a

473
00:30:55,318 --> 00:30:58,620
church at one time since Clement Danes its name was.'

474
00:30:58,620 --> 00:31:01,920
He smiled apologetically, as though conscious of saying something slightly

475
00:31:01,920 --> 00:31:07,318
ridiculous, and added: '"Oranges and lemons," say the bells of Saint Clements

476
00:31:07,318 --> 00:31:12,180
What's that?' said Winston. 'Oh—"' —"'Oranges and lemons,' say the bells of Saint Clements

477
00:31:12,180 --> 00:31:15,239
That was a rhyme we had when I was a little boy.

478
00:31:15,239 --> 00:31:17,159
How it goes on I don't remember, but I do

479
00:31:17,159 --> 00:31:21,898
know how it ended up, "Here comes a candle to light you to bed, Here comes a chopper

480
00:31:21,898 --> 00:31:23,520
to chop off your head."

481
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:25,260
It was a kind of dance.

482
00:31:25,260 --> 00:31:28,978
They held out their arms for you to pass under, and when they came to "Here comes a

483
00:31:28,978 --> 00:31:32,700
chopper to chop off your head" they brought their arms down and caught you.

484
00:31:32,700 --> 00:31:37,200
It was just names of churches. All the London churches were in it— —all the

485
00:31:37,200 --> 00:31:40,020
principal ones, that is.'

486
00:31:40,020 --> 00:31:43,379
Winston wandered vaguely to what century the church belonged.

487
00:31:43,379 --> 00:31:46,799
It was always difficult to determine the age of a London building.

488
00:31:46,799 --> 00:31:50,700
Anything large and impressive, if it was reasonably new in appearance, was

489
00:31:50,700 --> 00:31:54,000
automatically claimed as having been built since the Revolution, while

490
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:57,600
anything that was obviously of earlier date was ascribed to some dim period

491
00:31:57,600 --> 00:31:59,279
called the Middle Ages.

492
00:31:59,279 --> 00:32:03,959
The centuries of capitalism were held to have produced nothing of any value.

493
00:32:03,959 --> 00:32:08,639
One could not learn history from architecture any more than one could learn it from books.

494
00:32:08,639 --> 00:32:13,439
Statues, inscriptions, memorial stones, the names of streets— —anything that might

495
00:32:13,439 --> 00:32:17,639
throw light upon the past had been systematically altered.

496
00:32:17,639 --> 00:32:20,340
'I never knew it had been a church,' he said.

497
00:32:20,340 --> 00:32:23,879
'There's a lot of them left, really,' said the old man, 'though they've been put to

498
00:32:23,879 --> 00:32:27,540
other uses. Now, how did that rhyme go? Ah!

499
00:32:27,540 --> 00:32:28,920
I've got it!

500
00:32:28,920 --> 00:32:33,299
'Oranges and lemons,' say the bells of Saint Clemens You owe me three farthings,'

501
00:32:33,299 --> 00:32:37,559
say the bells was and Martins then that's as far as I can get. A

502
00:32:37,559 --> 00:32:41,700
farthing, that was a small copper coin, looked something like a cent.'

503
00:32:41,700 --> 00:32:46,920
'Where was Saint Martin's?' said Winston said Martins That's still standing. It's

504
00:32:46,920 --> 00:32:50,639
in Victory Square, alongside the picture gallery. A building with a kind of

505
00:32:50,639 --> 00:32:54,840
triangular porch and pillars in front, and a big flight of steps.'

506
00:32:54,840 --> 00:32:59,818
Winston knew the place well. It was a museum used for propaganda displays of

507
00:32:59,818 --> 00:33:04,799
various kinds— —scale models of rocket bombs and Floating Fortresses waxwork

508
00:33:04,799 --> 00:33:10,139
tableau's illustrating enemy atrocities, and the like Saint Martin's in the fields it used to

509
00:33:10,139 --> 00:33:14,159
be called,' supplemented the old man, 'though I don't recollect any fields

510
00:33:14,159 --> 00:33:17,059
anywhere in those parts.'

511
00:33:17,100 --> 00:33:21,059
Winston did not buy the picture. It would have been an even more incongruous

512
00:33:21,059 --> 00:33:24,779
position than the glass paperweight, and impossible to carry home, unless it were

513
00:33:24,779 --> 00:33:26,520
taken out of its frame.

514
00:33:26,520 --> 00:33:30,000
But he lingered for some minutes more, talking to the old man, whose name, he

515
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:33,540
discovered, was not Weeks— —as one might have gathered from the inscription over

516
00:33:33,540 --> 00:33:36,120
the shop front but Charrington.

517
00:33:36,120 --> 00:33:42,600
Mr Charrington, it seemed, was a widower aged 63 and had inhabited this shop for 30 years.

518
00:33:42,600 --> 00:33:45,719
Throughout that time he had been intending to alter the name over the

519
00:33:45,719 --> 00:33:49,020
window, but had never quite got to the point of doing it.

520
00:33:49,020 --> 00:33:51,959
All the while that they were talking the half-remembered rhyme kept running

521
00:33:51,959 --> 00:33:53,760
through Winston's head.

522
00:33:53,760 --> 00:33:58,020
Oranges and lemons, say the bells of Saint Clements You owe me three

523
00:33:58,020 --> 00:34:02,579
farthings, say the bells were sent Martins It was curious, but when you said it to

524
00:34:02,579 --> 00:34:06,000
yourself you had the illusion of actually hearing bells, the bells of a

525
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:10,739
lost London that still existed somewhere or other, disguised and forgotten.

526
00:34:10,739 --> 00:34:15,420
From one ghostly steeple after another he seemed to hear them peeling forth.

527
00:34:15,420 --> 00:34:21,359
Yet so far as he could remember he had never in real life heard church bells ringing.

528
00:34:21,359 --> 00:34:25,680
He got away from Mr Charrington and went down the stairs alone, so as not to let

529
00:34:25,680 --> 00:34:29,760
the old man see him reconnoiting the street before stepping out of the door.

530
00:34:29,760 --> 00:34:34,139
He had already made up his mind that after a suitable interval— —a month, say— —he

531
00:34:34,139 --> 00:34:36,420
would take the risk of visiting the shop again.

532
00:34:36,420 --> 00:34:40,620
It was perhaps not more dangerous than shirking an evening at the center

533
00:34:40,620 --> 00:34:44,100
a serious piece of folly had been to come back here in the first place, after

534
00:34:44,100 --> 00:34:48,540
buying the diary and without knowing whether the proprietor of the shop could be trusted.

535
00:34:48,540 --> 00:34:50,700
However———!

536
00:34:50,700 --> 00:34:52,500
Yes, he thought again, he would come back.

537
00:34:52,500 --> 00:34:55,500
He would buy further scraps of beautiful rubbish.

538
00:34:55,500 --> 00:34:58,978
He would buy the engraving of Saint Clement Danes take it out of its frame

539
00:34:58,978 --> 00:35:03,420
and carry it home concealed under the jacket of his overalls. He would drag the

540
00:35:03,420 --> 00:35:06,600
rest of that poem out of Mr Charrington's memory.

541
00:35:06,600 --> 00:35:10,559
Even the lunatic project of renting the room upstairs flashed momentarily

542
00:35:10,559 --> 00:35:12,180
through his mind again.

543
00:35:12,180 --> 00:35:16,978
For perhaps five seconds exaltation made him careless, and he stepped out onto the

544
00:35:16,978 --> 00:35:20,639
pavement without so much as a preliminary glance through the window.

545
00:35:20,639 --> 00:35:25,799
He had even started humming to an improvised tune— 'Oranges and lemon say

546
00:35:25,799 --> 00:35:31,020
the bells of Saint claim and You owe me three farthings,' say that

547
00:35:31,020 --> 00:35:34,978
Suddenly his heart seemed to turn to ice and his bowels to water.

548
00:35:34,978 --> 00:35:39,959
A figure in blue overalls was coming down the pavement, not 10 meters away.

549
00:35:39,959 --> 00:35:43,799
It was the girl from the Fiction Department, the girl with dark hair.

550
00:35:43,799 --> 00:35:47,760
The light was failing, but there was no difficulty in recognizing her.

551
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:53,639
She looked him straight in the face, then walked quickly on as though she had not seen him.

552
00:35:53,639 --> 00:35:57,120
For a few seconds Winston was too paralyzed to move.

553
00:35:57,120 --> 00:36:00,898
Then he turned to the right and walked heavily away, not noticing for the moment

554
00:36:00,898 --> 00:36:03,239
that he was going in the wrong direction.

555
00:36:03,239 --> 00:36:05,219
At any rate, one question was settled.

556
00:36:05,219 --> 00:36:08,939
There was no doubting any longer that the girl was spying on him.

557
00:36:08,939 --> 00:36:12,840
She must have followed him here, because it was not credible that by pure chance

558
00:36:12,840 --> 00:36:16,260
she should have happened to be walking on the same evening up the same obscure

559
00:36:16,260 --> 00:36:21,420
Backstreet kilometers distance from any quarter where Party members lived.

560
00:36:21,420 --> 00:36:23,818
It was too great a coincidence.

561
00:36:23,818 --> 00:36:27,659
Whether she was really an agent of the Thought Police, or simply an amateur spy

562
00:36:27,659 --> 00:36:30,359
actuated by officiousness, hardly mattered.

563
00:36:30,359 --> 00:36:32,700
It was enough that she was watching him.

564
00:36:32,700 --> 00:36:36,779
Probably she had seen him go into the pub as well.

565
00:36:36,779 --> 00:36:40,739
It was an effort to walk. The lump of glass in his pocket banged against his

566
00:36:40,739 --> 00:36:45,540
thigh at each step, and he was half-minded to take it out and throw it away.

567
00:36:45,540 --> 00:36:49,260
The worst thing was the pain in his belly.

568
00:36:49,260 --> 00:36:52,200
For a couple of minutes he had the feeling that he would die if he did not

569
00:36:52,200 --> 00:36:53,939
reach a lavatory soon.

570
00:36:53,939 --> 00:36:58,200
But there would be no public lavatories in a quarter like this. Then the spasm

571
00:36:58,200 --> 00:37:01,799
past leaving a dull ache behind.

572
00:37:01,799 --> 00:37:05,639
The street was a blind alley. Winston Halton stood for several seconds

573
00:37:05,639 --> 00:37:10,920
wondering vaguely what to do, then turned round and began to retrace his steps.

574
00:37:10,920 --> 00:37:14,100
As he turned it occurred to him that the girl had only passed him three minutes

575
00:37:14,100 --> 00:37:17,939
ago and that by running he could probably catch up with her.

576
00:37:17,939 --> 00:37:21,779
He could keep on her track till they were in some quiet place, and then smash

577
00:37:21,779 --> 00:37:24,478
her skull in with a cobblestone.

578
00:37:24,478 --> 00:37:27,959
The piece of glass in his pocket would be heavy enough for the job.

579
00:37:27,959 --> 00:37:31,978
But he abandoned the idea immediately, because even the thought of making any

580
00:37:31,978 --> 00:37:36,540
physical effort was unbearable. He could not run, he could not strike a blow.

581
00:37:36,540 --> 00:37:40,139
Besides, she was young and lusty and would defend herself.

582
00:37:40,139 --> 00:37:43,500
He thought also of hurrying to the Community center and staying there till

583
00:37:43,500 --> 00:37:47,520
the place closed, so as to establish a partial alibi for the evening.

584
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:49,739
But that too was impossible.

585
00:37:49,739 --> 00:37:52,559
A deadly lassitude had taken hold of him.

586
00:37:52,559 --> 00:37:57,779
All he wanted was to get home quickly and then sit down and be quiet.

587
00:37:57,779 --> 00:38:02,219
It was after 22 hours when he got back to the flat. The lights would be switched

588
00:38:02,219 --> 00:38:06,478
off at the main at 23 30. He went into the kitchen and swallowed nearly a

589
00:38:06,478 --> 00:38:08,579
teacup full of Victory Gin.

590
00:38:08,579 --> 00:38:12,659
Then he went to the table in the alcove, sat down and took the diary out of the

591
00:38:12,659 --> 00:38:13,799
drawer.

592
00:38:13,799 --> 00:38:15,959
But he did not open it at once.

593
00:38:15,959 --> 00:38:20,520
From the telescreen a brassy female voice was squalling a patriotic song.

594
00:38:20,520 --> 00:38:24,719
He sat staring at the marbled cover of the book, trying without success to shut

595
00:38:24,719 --> 00:38:27,959
the voice out of his consciousness.

596
00:38:27,959 --> 00:38:32,579
It was at night that they came for you, always at night. The proper thing was to

597
00:38:32,579 --> 00:38:34,439
kill yourself before they got you.

598
00:38:34,439 --> 00:38:36,959
Undoubtedly some people did so.

599
00:38:36,959 --> 00:38:41,760
Many of the disappearances were actually suicides. But it needed desperate courage

600
00:38:41,760 --> 00:38:45,180
to kill yourself in a world where firearms, or any quick and certain poison,

601
00:38:45,180 --> 00:38:48,059
were completely unprocurable.

602
00:38:48,059 --> 00:38:51,719
He thought with the kind of astonishment of the biological uselessness of pain

603
00:38:51,719 --> 00:38:56,040
and fear, the treachery of the human body which always freezes into inertia at

604
00:38:56,040 --> 00:38:59,579
exactly the moment when a special effort is needed.

605
00:38:59,579 --> 00:39:04,500
He might have silenced the dark-haired girl if only he had acted quickly enough:

606
00:39:04,500 --> 00:39:09,420
but precisely because of the extremity of his danger he had lost the power to act.

607
00:39:09,420 --> 00:39:13,020
It struck him that in moments of crisis one is never fighting against an

608
00:39:13,020 --> 00:39:16,500
external enemy, but always against one's own body.

609
00:39:16,500 --> 00:39:22,200
Even now, in spite of the Jinn the Dal Lake in his belly made consecutive thought impossible.

610
00:39:22,200 --> 00:39:27,000
And it is the same, he perceived, in all seemingly heroic or tragic situations.

611
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:31,139
On the battlefield, in the torture chamber, on a sinking ship, the issues

612
00:39:31,139 --> 00:39:34,680
that you are fighting for are always forgotten, because the body swells up

613
00:39:34,680 --> 00:39:38,639
until it fills the universe, and even when you are not paralyzed by fright or

614
00:39:38,639 --> 00:39:42,898
screaming with pain, life is a moment to moment struggle against hunger or cold

615
00:39:42,898 --> 00:39:48,239
or sleeplessness, against a sour stomach or an aching tooth.

616
00:39:48,239 --> 00:39:53,459
He opened the diary. It was important to write something down. The woman on the

617
00:39:53,459 --> 00:39:57,540
telescreen had started a new song. Her voice seemed to stick into his brain

618
00:39:57,540 --> 00:39:59,818
like jagged splinters of glass.

619
00:39:59,818 --> 00:40:04,620
He tried to think of O'Brien, for whom, or to whom, the diary was written, but

620
00:40:04,620 --> 00:40:07,559
instead he began thinking of the things that would happen to him after the

621
00:40:07,559 --> 00:40:09,659
Thought Police took him away.

622
00:40:09,659 --> 00:40:14,639
It would not matter if they killed you at once. To be killed was what you expected.

623
00:40:14,639 --> 00:40:19,379
But before death (nobody spoke of such things, yet everybody knew of them) there

624
00:40:19,379 --> 00:40:23,100
was the routine of confession that had to be gone through: the groveling on the

625
00:40:23,100 --> 00:40:27,540
floor and screaming for mercy, the crack of broken bones, the smashed teeth and

626
00:40:27,540 --> 00:40:30,620
the bloody clots of hair.

627
00:40:30,659 --> 00:40:35,219
Why did you have to endure it, since the end was always the same? Why was it not

628
00:40:35,219 --> 00:40:38,100
possible to cut a few days or weeks out of your life?

629
00:40:38,100 --> 00:40:42,719
Nobody ever escaped detection, and nobody ever failed to confess.

630
00:40:42,719 --> 00:40:46,318
When once you had succumbed to thought crime it was certain that by a given

631
00:40:46,318 --> 00:40:48,359
date you would be dead.

632
00:40:48,359 --> 00:40:54,559
Why then did that horror, which altered nothing, have to lie embedded in future time?

633
00:40:54,659 --> 00:40:59,520
He tried with a little more success than before to summon up the image of O'Brien.

634
00:40:59,520 --> 00:41:03,779
'We shall meet in the place where there is no darkness,' O'Brien had said to him.

635
00:41:03,779 --> 00:41:06,420
He knew what it meant, or thought he knew.

636
00:41:06,420 --> 00:41:10,439
The place where there is no darkness was the imagined future, which one would

637
00:41:10,439 --> 00:41:15,239
never see, but which, by foreknowledge, one could mystically share in.

638
00:41:15,239 --> 00:41:18,959
But with the voice from the telescreen nagging at his ears he could not follow

639
00:41:18,959 --> 00:41:21,239
the train of thought further.

640
00:41:21,239 --> 00:41:23,219
He put a cigarette in his mouth.

641
00:41:23,219 --> 00:41:27,239
Half the tobacco promptly fell out onto his tongue, a bit of dust which was

642
00:41:27,239 --> 00:41:29,700
difficult to spit out again.

643
00:41:29,700 --> 00:41:34,559
The face of Big Brother swam into his mind, displacing that of O'Brien.

644
00:41:34,559 --> 00:41:38,579
Just as he had done a few days earlier, he slid a coin out of his pocket and

645
00:41:38,579 --> 00:41:44,520
looked at it. The face gazed up at him, heavy, calm, protecting: but what kind of

646
00:41:44,520 --> 00:41:50,879
smile was hidden beneath the dark mustache Like a lead and nil the words came back at him:

647
00:41:50,879 --> 00:41:56,000
WAR IS PEACE FREEDOM IS SLAVERY IGNORANCE IS STRENGTH
